{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'defn- program-env|::outcomes|unit-opts|continuation-uast|free-names|defined-keys' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc src/cljc/yang/core/prelude.cljc test/yang/clojure/stream_eval_test.cljc; sed -n '2220,2255p;2940,2990p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc; sed -n '1,100p' test/yang/clojure/stream_eval_test.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:861:  (some? (::outcomes env)))\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:949:(def ^:private continuation-uast\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2118:(defn- program-env\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2165:            (:outcomes opts) (assoc ::outcomes {:adapters (or (:adapters opts) {})}))))))\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2422:                             (discard-result continuation-uast))\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2522:   map stays for the `library-opts`/`unit-opts` fixtures and the prelude's\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2968:(def unit-opts\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:3083:   the guest library they need, compiled unmodified under `unit-opts`\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:3098:         program (compile-program (concat source library) true unit-opts)\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/prelude.cljc:79:(defn free-names\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/prelude.cljc:99:(defn defined-keys\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/prelude.cljc:115:                (remove (conj (defined-keys ast) 'yin/def) (free-names ast)))))\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/prelude.cljc:144:  [{:keys [module-name ast exports requires free-names skip? registry message\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/prelude.cljc:158:         free-names)})\n(defn compile-program\n  \"Compile a sequence of Clojure forms into a single Universal AST.\n   Top-level defs compile to explicit yin/def store writes, sequenced via lambda.\n   Top-level defmacros are lowered to ordinary (yin/def name (fn ...)) syntax\n   with :macro? true on the lambda; the encoder turns that flag into a\n   declaration row and the expander, not this compiler, recognizes calls.\n   The three-argument form takes `opts` opting into the argument-vector\n   convention (see `program-env`); without it the output is the native one.\"\n  ([forms] (compile-program forms true))\n  ([forms tail?] (compile-program forms tail? nil))\n  ([forms tail? opts]\n   (run! check-program-symbols! forms)\n   (let [env (program-env forms opts)]\n     ;; A discarded definition must not become a lexical binding in the\n     ;; following definitions. Descriptor entry and body closures both capture\n     ;; their environment: retaining the previous descriptor here doubles its\n     ;; paths at each definition during machine-data validation and UCF lift.\n     (letfn [(discard-result\n               [ast]\n               (if (outcomes? env) (u/then ast (u/lit nil)) ast))\n             (go\n               [forms macro-env]\n               (if (empty? forms)\n                 (compile-literal nil tail?)\n                 (let [head       (cond->> (first forms)\n                                    (outcomes? env) (lower-top-form env macro-env))\n                       rest-forms (rest forms)]\n                   (cond\n                     ;; defmacro: ordinary (yin/def name (fn ...)) syntax whose\n                     ;; lambda the encoder declares; later forms see the name\n                     ;; only so the native defn lowering can yield to it.\n                     (and (seq? head) (= 'defmacro (first head)))\n                     (let [defined (compile-defmacro (rest head) env false macro-env)]\n                       (cond-> {:type     :application\n                                :operator {:type :lambda, :params ['_]\n                                           :body (go rest-forms\n  [form]\n  ;; the single-form entry runs the same reserved-prefix pre-pass as\n  ;; `compile-program`, so a REPL one-liner can no more spell a\n  ;; compiler-private (`dsg__`) name than a whole program can. Trusted\n  ;; compiler construction never reaches `compile`: it builds its own\n  ;; `dsg__` temporaries via `compile-form` directly.\n  (check-program-symbols! form)\n  (compile-form form true {}))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The linked runtime entry (the conformance matrix round, yang.antlr.md\n;; section 8.10)\n;; =============================================================================\n;;\n;; A linked clojure program is published as an ordinary linker module whose\n;; one export, `body`, is its unmodified lowered unit, and run under its own\n;; entry, `clj/run-main` (the `clj` runtime module, yang.clojure.prelude).\n;; `read-forms` is the frontend's parser — the reader itself — and\n;; `lower-unit` lowers what it reads to the entry's argument: one thunk\n;; whose call runs the program compiled under the argument-vector\n;; convention and the outcome model over the guest library, with every\n;; top-level definition published to the runtime global namespace\n;; (`clj/define-global!`/`clj/global-get`), as a Python def lands in\n;; its module's globals. No call node is rewritten and no foreign\n;; `:code` is extracted: the unit is the program's own lowering,\n;; wrapped.\n\n(def unit-opts\n  \"The `compile-program` opts a linked unit compiles with: the\n   argument-vector convention with the outcome model over the guest\n   library's adapters, every VM primitive callable directly by bare name.\"\n  {:convention :arg-vector,\n   :outcomes true,\n   :adapters library-adapters,\n   :native vm/primitive-profiles})\n\n\n(def top-defining-operators\n  \"The operators whose second element names a top-level definition.\"\n  '#{def defn defn- defmacro})\n\n\n(defn read-forms\n  \"Every form `source` text reads. The reader is the parser: a malformed\n   source throws, as any frontend's refused parse does.\n\n   Source-string compilation is a JVM/Node capability (tools.reader is\n   an mvn dependency, absent from the jolt host's source-root classpath\n   — the jolt lane loads compiled output and ships source strings to\n   remote evaluators, so this is unreachable there; the honest error\n(ns yang.clojure.stream-eval-test\n  \"yang.clojure stream programs on yin.vm, evaluated through the yin.repl\n   shell, which is the composition around dao.stream media: each program\n   is compiled by yang.clojure, appended to the program medium as one row\n   batch, expanded onto the row medium, loaded and run by the evaluator's\n   observer, and answered on the output medium as printed chunks followed\n   by one result token (yin.repl; docs/design/dao.stream.md).  The streams\n   a program makes -- `stream/make`, `put!`, `cursor`, `next!`, `close!`\n   -- are ring buffers the shell hands the VM as `:make-stream`, so every\n   stream a program touches is a dao.stream medium.\n\n   Every case runs on the four evaluators the shell composes, with the\n   value texts pinned, so the cases double as a cross-VM parity corpus.\"\n  (:require [dao.test-slow :as slow] [clojure.string :as str]\n            [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ring]\n            [yin.repl :as repl]\n            [yin.repl.frontends :as repl.frontends]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Harness\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def vm-types\n  \"Every evaluator the shell composes (`yin.repl/vm-constructors`).\"\n  [:ast-walker :semantic :stack :register])\n\n\n(defn- session\n  \"A fresh shell on `vm-type`, optionally around a caller-owned output\n   medium.\"\n  ([vm-type] (session vm-type nil))\n  ([vm-type output]\n   (repl.frontends/create-state (cond-> {:vm-type vm-type}\n                                  output (assoc :output-stream output)))))\n\n\n(defn- evaluate\n  \"Evaluate `lines` in order against one threaded state; `[state texts]`.\n   The shell is synchronous, so no driver is needed.  The program media are\n   shared by every state threaded from one session, so a state is never\n   evaluated against twice.\"\n  [state lines]\n  (reduce (fn [[state texts] line]\n            (let [[state' text] (repl/eval-input state line)]\n              [state' (conj texts text)]))\n          [state []]\n          lines))\n\n\n(defn- texts\n  [vm-type lines]\n  (second (evaluate (session vm-type) lines)))\n\n\n(defn- ring-handle\n  [capacity]\n  (:dao.stream/handle\n    (ring/create! {:dao.stream/type ring/transport-type\n                   ring/capacity-key capacity})))\n\n\n(defn- read-all\n  \"Every element on `handle` from its oldest, then `[:end outcome]` for\n   the outcome that ended the read.\"\n  [handle]\n  (loop [cursor (:dao.stream/cursor\n                  (stream/cursor handle :dao.stream/oldest))\n         elements []]\n    (let [result (stream/next handle cursor)]\n      (if (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome result))\n        (recur (:dao.stream/cursor result)\n               (conj elements (:dao.stream/value result)))\n        (conj elements [:end (:dao.stream/outcome result)])))))\n\n\n(defn- stream-ref?\n  \"A rendered stream reference; hosts print map keys in their own order.\"\n  [text]\n  (and (str/starts-with? text \"{\")\n       (str/includes? text \":type :stream-ref\")))\n\n\n(defn- error?\n  [text]\n  (str/starts-with? text \"Error: \"))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Pure expressions and let bindings\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def pure-corpus\n  \"`[source expected]` pairs: literals, primitives, conditionals, lambdas,\n   and let bindings, each answered as the shell renders the value.\"\n  [[\"42\" \"42\"]\n   [\"\\\"hi\\\"\" \"\\\"hi\\\"\"]\n   [\":k\" \":k\"]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"The Clojure prelude already exports `clj/rt-ctx`. The difference is that linked Clojure units also emit a second, inline continuation family that reads `clj.rt/ctx` directly; sibling units use their required runtime’s exported operations. I’m switching linked units to the existing Clojure runtime family while retaining inline emission for standalone programs."}}
